# Visualize Plastid Genome Structure

## Lab Questions and Answers

## 1. What is the scientific name of your chosen plant and its plastid genome accession number?

The scientific name of my chosen plant is *Glycine soja*, specifically isolate HAAS216. Its NCBI GenBank accession number is PX591265.1.

## 2. What is the total length of the plastid genome in base pairs?

The total length of the chloroplast genome is 152,222 base pairs (bp).

## 3. Can you identify the LSC, SSC, IRa, and IRb regions on your map? Briefly describe their positions.

The LSC is the large single-copy region, while the SSC is the smaller single-copy region. IRa and IRb are the two inverted repeat regions that contain duplicated parts of the genome.

## 4. Give three examples of genes located in the LSC region.

Three examples are *psbA*, *rbcL*, and *atpB*. These genes are involved in photosynthesis and other important chloroplast functions. Their exact locations should be checked against the genome map.

## 5. Give at least one example of a gene located in the SSC region.

One example is ndhF. This gene is associated with the NADH dehydrogenase-like complex and plays a role in photosynthetic electron transport.

## 6. Give at least one example of a gene that occurs within an inverted repeat region. Is the gene shown more than once because of the duplicated IR regions?

One example is ndhB. It can appear twice on the genome map because the two inverted repeat regions contain duplicated copies of part of the genome.

## 7. Choose one photosynthesis-related gene visible on your map. State its gene name and its general biological function.

I chose psbA. This gene codes for the D1 protein of Photosystem II, which is important in the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis.

## 8. What does the direction of the gene arrows or gene orientation tell you about transcription on the plastid genome?

The arrows show the direction in which the genes are transcribed. Genes pointing in different directions are transcribed from different DNA strands. This helps show how the genes are arranged around the chloroplast genome.

## 9. Look at the GC content graph. Does GC content appear uniform across the entire plastid genome, or does it vary among regions? Describe what you observe without over-interpreting it.

The overall GC content of my sequence is about 35.37%. The graph can be used to observe whether GC content changes across different parts of the genome. I would describe the pattern based on the graph without assuming what causes the differences.

## 10. Why is a graphical plastid genome map useful compared with viewing only the raw DNA sequence or a long list of annotations?

A graphical plastid genome map is useful because it makes the genome easier to understand. It shows the major regions, gene locations, gene directions, and GC-content pattern in one figure. It is easier to see the overall organization this way than by reading a long DNA sequence or list of annotations.
